  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.3. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to engage in a forward-looking international labour standards policy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Office received all article 22 reports on Conventions nos. 100, C 111 and C 188 due by the deadline. In 2021, the ILO provided technical assistance to the Ministry of Labour (MOL) in coordinating an integrated response to its reporting obligations on the ILO Conventions especially Convention No. 111, and the first report on Convention 188. Training sessions also included the application of ratified ILO Conventions by Thailand.  In November 2020, the ILO also provided technical assistance to Thailand&apos;s Multi-disciplinary Committee on Actions Against Child Labour and its Worse Forms to ensure that all relevant agencies have the same and better understanding of the definitions of child labour, working children and light work in line with the ILO&apos;s definitions specified in ILO Conventions related to child labour. This resulted in a better statistical collection for making an informed decision in the formulation of the National Action Plan on Elimination of Child Labour in Thailand.   In January 2020, the ILO provided technical expertise to the MOL so as to meet its obligation and reply to the CEACR&apos;s direct request on Convention No. 127 which Thailand ratified.  This has helped the authorities to collect information among stakeholders before considering the amendment of related laws and regulations.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">2.3.1. Percentage of reports on the application of ratified Conventions due by 1 September received in a timely manner that include replies to comments of the supervisory bodies</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.2. Increased capacity of the member States to apply international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">&quot;The Committee notes with satisfaction that section 53 of the Labour Protection Act was amended in 2019 (B.E. 2562/2019) so as to prescribe that an employer shall set equal rates of wage, overtime pay, holiday pay, and holiday overtime pay between men and women for &quot;work of equal value&quot;. The Office provided technical assistance on many occasions to get to the specific result of legislative change on equal pay for work of equal value, as far back as the time of ratification by Thailand of Convention 100. Assistance provided has consisted of repeated advice in series of formats, including translation and publication of materials on the elimination of discrimination at work, replies to written questions and presentations in workshops, some of which were directly  attended by ILO experts.
During 2020-21, the Office provided information and proposals through, at least 3-4, informal meetings and consultations held with Government officials.  These meetings dealt,  inter alia, with non-discrimination and equal treatment issues.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">2.2.1. Number of cases of progress in the application of ratified Conventions noted with satisfaction by the supervisory bodies</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
